      "headline": "A Bizarre Accounting Method",
      "keyInsight": "Most people judge an action purely by its final outcome. If you pull a reckless stunt and walk away with a million dollars, society calls you a brilliant winner.\n\nBut wise accounting requires looking at the ghost worlds that could have happened instead. Think of Russian roulette.\n\nIf someone offers you ten million dollars to pull the trigger on a revolver with one bullet, and you survive, did you make a smart trade? Common sense says no, because in five other parallel branches of reality, you ended up in a grave.\n\nA dollar earned through blind gamble is poisoned by the unseen disaster lurking right beside it. Real value must always be discounted by the catastrophe that almost occurred.",
      "content": "Imagine an investor sitting at a desk with an odd balance sheet. On the left side, he writes down the cash sitting in his safe. But on the right side, he tallies invisible ghosts.\n\nThese ghosts are alternative histories: all the events that were entirely possible, but simply did not occur. Most people only count the single history that actually took place. If an entrepreneur gambles the entire firm on a coin flip and wins, everybody toasts their sharp business vision.\n\nNow consider an eccentric billionaire who offers a volunteer ten million dollars to play Russian roulette with a six-chamber revolver. The brave fool puts the gun to his head, pulls the trigger, and hears a dull click. He walks away carrying a briefcase stuffed with crisp hundred-dollar bills.\n\nHe buys a luxury yacht and a lavish penthouse. A naive observer sees an enviable, self-made tycoon. But in five out of six parallel realities, that same man is dead on the floor.\n\nThat briefcase of cash is not clean wealth. It is toxic money, heavily stained by the disastrous path that missed him by a hair's breadth. Compare him to a diligent dentist who earns a modest living through forty years of gentle tooth repairs.\n\nThe dentist had zero chance of waking up dead on any given Tuesday. When measuring success, you cannot just look at the prize in hand. You must subtract the deadly bullets that failed to fire.",
