      "id": "fooled-by-randomness-ch-6",
      "order": 6,
      "chapterNumber": 6,
      "headline": "The Asymmetry of Falling Off a Cliff",
      "keyInsight": "Most people confuse probability with expectation. They ask: \"Is an event likely to happen?\" That is the wrong question.\n\nThe right question is: \"If it happens, how much does it cost me?\" You can be right 999 times out of a thousand, yet still end up bankrupt on the thousandth try. Imagine picking up pennies in front of a giant steamroller.\n\nMost seconds, you pocket free money and smile. But one tiny trip flattens you into a pancake.\n\nProbability is symmetric only in math textbooks. In the real world, consequences are wildly lopsided.\n\nBeing mostly right is completely useless if being wrong is fatal.",
      "content": "Picture crossing a quiet country bridge every morning. Ninety-nine percent of the time, the crossing is smooth, quick, and scenic. But if the bridge collapses one morning out of a thousand, the average outcome is not \"mostly safe.\" You only have one life to lose.\n\nA tiny chance of catastrophe instantly overrules a massive pile of minor victories.\n\nTaleb points out that human intuition expects symmetry. We naturally assume that if something works nine times out of ten, we should bet heavily on it. But rare events break this symmetry completely.\n\nAn investor might boast about making money on eighty percent of trading days. If the small twenty percent loss wipes out all past gains, that high win rate is pure optical illusion.\n\nWise thinkers focus on consequences rather than odds. When Taleb makes an investment, he is happy being wrong dozens of times on tiny stakes if a single win pays off a thousandfold. Protect your downside from total ruin, and let rare windfalls take care of the rest.\n\nSurvival depends on respecting the steep cliff, not counting the flat steps before it.",
